        <section id="architecture-decisions" class="section architecture-decisions">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">07 / ARCHITECTURE &amp; ENGINEERING DECISIONS</p>
          <h2>Architecture follows the problem, not the technology.</h2>
          <p>
            Each flagship platform was designed around a different data engineering problem,
            with technology choices driven by workload characteristics, reliability,
            scalability, maintainability and analytical requirements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="decision-grid">
          <article class="decision-card">
            <span class="decision-number">01</span>
            <h3>Real-Time Data Platform</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Problem:</strong> Process high-volume vehicle telemetry continuously
              while supporting downstream APIs, analytics and AI workloads.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Architecture:</strong> Kafka and streaming consumers provide the
              event-driven ingestion path, with PostgreSQL supporting operational access
              and a FastAPI product layer exposing validated data and model results.
            </p>
            <div class="architecture-visual" aria-label="Project 01 architecture flow">
              <div class="flow-label">Architecture flow</div>
              <div class="flow-track">
                <span class="flow-node">EV Telemetry</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">FastAPI</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">Kafka</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">Streaming</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node highlight">PostgreSQL / AI / API</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              <strong>Engineering reasoning:</strong> Streaming architecture was chosen
              because telemetry arrives continuously and requires decoupled ingestion,
              processing and downstream consumption.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="decision-card">
            <span class="decision-number">02</span>
            <h3>Cloud-Native Lakehouse</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Problem:</strong> Build a replayable cloud data platform that handles
              raw data, incremental changes, CDC, governed storage and analytical access.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Architecture:</strong> S3, Parquet, Iceberg, Glue, Athena and
              Terraform form the cloud platform, supported by PostgreSQL CDC and controlled
              ingestion and transformation layers.
            </p>
            <div class="architecture-visual" aria-label="Project 02 architecture flow">
              <div class="flow-label">Architecture flow</div>
              <div class="flow-track">
                <span class="flow-node">PostgreSQL</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">CDC</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">S3 / Parquet</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">Iceberg</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node highlight">Glue / Athena</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              <strong>Engineering reasoning:</strong> The lakehouse pattern separates
              immutable raw data from managed analytical layers while supporting
              incremental processing, governance and replayability.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="decision-card">
            <span class="decision-number">03</span>
            <h3>Modern Data Warehouse</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Problem:</strong> Transform operational data into trusted analytical
              structures that business users and BI products can consume efficiently.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Architecture:</strong> Raw and staging layers feed dimensional models,
              incremental transformations, analytics engineering models, data marts and
              BI-ready products.
            </p>
            <div class="architecture-visual" aria-label="Project 03 architecture flow">
              <div class="flow-label">Architecture flow</div>
              <div class="flow-track">
                <span class="flow-node">Raw Data</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">Staging</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">Dimensional Models</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node">ELT / Marts</span>
                <b class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</b>
                <span class="flow-node highlight">Semantic / BI</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              <strong>Engineering reasoning:</strong> Warehouse and dimensional modeling
              were used to establish consistent business entities, reusable metrics,
              analytical performance and controlled downstream consumption.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="decision-principles">
          <div>
            <strong>Design principle</strong>
            <span>Choose architecture from workload and data characteristics.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Reliability principle</strong>
            <span>Validate data and system behavior instead of relying on assumptions.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Engineering principle</strong>
            <span>Prefer maintainable, observable and testable systems over unnecessary complexity.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
